\chapter{JSON, YAML, TOML, and INI}
\label{ch:53}
\index{JSON, YAML, TOML, and INI}

The format libraries expose focused source operations backed by MuLIB C bridges. They pass supported scalars across the bridge and use bounded snapshots or status tuples where appropriate. JSON compiler intrinsics additionally have the lexical owner/view rules in \cref{ch:28}. General foreign handles are outside both the scalar bridge surface and the safe imports available from reviewed contracts.

\section{Preserve format errors and absence}
When reading a setting, test an absent key, a present empty string, a valid nonempty string, a malformed document, and a valid document with a value of the wrong kind. Keep the wrapper's status separate from the returned text. A fallback value alone can conflate all five outcomes. Choose the documented snapshot or lifetime for each returned value and check the limits of a large document separately. The standard-library extension example exercises the concrete bridge and build plan. A link failure should lead you to the build helper's selected bridge groups before editing the parser call.

\section{Read a worker count in two stages}
The extension example first asks INI for the text under section \OnxCodeTerm{app} and key \OnxCodeTerm{workers}. Only a zero status permits the decimal scan. With the example value 42 the scan succeeds and its next offset equals the length of the text, so the program prints the count. Change just the value to \OnxCodeTerm{42x}. The configuration lookup is still successful, and the integer scanner can still recognize 42, but its offset points at the unconsumed letter. The example reports an invalid count. Complete consumption is a property the application adds to the prefix scanner.

Now compare a line containing an empty value with a document that omits the workers key. In the former case the lookup succeeds and the scanner has no digits; in the latter the bridge returns the missing-key status, one, and the scanner should never run. For duplicate keys the bridge copies the first matching value and continues validating the rest of the document. It cannot safely hand the caller a pointer to transient callback storage or declare the file valid merely because it found the key early.

The independent normalization call at the end feeds invalid JSON to a different parser and expects status minus two. That number denotes parser rejection in the bridge contract; it is not the INI missing-key code. Storing the tuple returned by each wrapper keeps the outcome associated with the right text even when another operation replaces the underlying bridge status.

Try a negative worker count as well. The signed scanner can consume it completely. If workers must be positive, a range check belongs after the complete-consumption test; neither parser enforces that application policy.

